        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best portable treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people, the WalkingPad Z1: a 51 lb walking pad with a 47.6-inch walking area that folds in half to 5.4 inches high, so it slides under many beds and sofas. If you need the lightest machine to carry, the UREVO Strol 2E Pro weighs about 43 lb. If you want to run, the 104 lb WalkingPad X25 reaches 10 mph and folds into a closet.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the lightest portable treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Of the machines whose weight we could confirm with two sources, the UREVO Strol 2E Pro is the lightest at about 43 lb. Some pads are listed lighter, such as UREVO's SpaceWalk E1L Pro at 35.5 lb on UREVO's figure, but other sources give a different weight and capacity. Very light pads also tend to have short belts.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can one person move a portable treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually, on a level floor. Pads under about 55 lb can be lifted at one end, rolled or carried a short way. Machines around 80 to 105 lb are folded, tipped onto their wheels and rolled. A 180 lb folding treadmill rolls across a hard floor but needs two people for steps or stairs. Small wheels struggle on rugs and thick carpet.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you run on a portable treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On most walking pads, no: they are walking machines with short belts. Folding 2-in-1s such as the WalkingPad R3 Hybrid+, at up to 7.5 mph, suit walking and light jogging, and the double-fold X25 reaches 10 mph on a 52-inch belt. For regular running on a longer deck, an auto-folding treadmill such as the Echelon Stride-S is heavier but better suited.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use a portable treadmill in an RV?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Possibly, with care. Choose a light machine you can secure flat while driving, check its power rating against your shore power, generator or inverter and the maker's guidance, and use it on a firm, level spot at walking pace. A manual treadmill avoids the power question entirely. No maker on this page markets its treadmill for vehicle use.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Will a portable treadmill fit under my bed?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if the gap is higher than the machine when stored. WalkingPad gives the Z1's folded height as 5.4 inches, so it needs a little more than that, measured at the lowest point of the bed frame. Double-fold treadmills are too thick for most beds and are designed to stand in a closet or corner instead. Always measure before you buy.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
